      </span>

      <h1>{title}</h1>
      <p class="meta"><time datetime="2026-07-01">Published July 1, 2026</time></p>

      <div set:html={`
<p>For years, Amazon's Kindle ecosystem was a one-way street. You bought a book from the Kindle Store, and it stayed inside Amazon's walled garden. Moving a Kindle purchase to a Kobo, a PocketBook, or a Boox device required stripping DRM — a gray-area process that most readers understandably wanted to avoid.</p> <p>In July 2026, Amazon quietly changed the equation. The Kindle Store now <strong>shows DRM-free status on book product pages</strong>, and a growing number of Kindle titles are available for download as DRM-free EPUB files. This is not the broad, store-wide DRM removal many readers have been asking for, but it is a real, verifiable step toward an open ebook ecosystem — and one that every EPUB reader should understand how to use.</p> <h2>What Changed: Amazon's DRM-Free Indicator Goes Live</h2> <p>Amazon announced in December 2025 that it would accept EPUB and PDF uploads from publishers and independent authors for distribution on the Kindle Store. The policy change for DRM, however, took until mid-2026 to surface in the storefront itself.</p> <p>Starting in late June 2026, Amazon began displaying a DRM-Free badge on eligible Kindle book product pages. Under the book's main summary, where you would normally see file size, print length, and publisher information, the page now reads:</p> <blockquote><p>&#8220;At the Publisher's request, this title is being sold without Digital Rights Management Software (DRM) applied.&#8221;</p></blockquote> <p>There is a second indicator you can check before buying. Open the <strong>"Product Details"</strong> section on any Kindle book page and look for the line that says <strong>"Simultaneous Device Usage: Unlimited"</strong>. This is a reliable signal that the book has no DRM lock, and you will be able to download it as a genuine EPUB file without restrictions.</p> <p>Both indicators are visible before you make a purchase. You do not need to buy the book first to verify its DRM status.</p> <h2>How to Find DRM-Free Kindle Books</h2> <p>DRM-free Kindle books are still the minority on the platform, but they are growing in number. Here is the most reliable way to find them:</p> <h3>Method 1: Check the Product Page Before Buying</h3> <p>When browsing the Kindle Store on Amazon.com, scroll down past the book description to the <strong>Product Details</strong> section. If you see any of these indicators, the book is DRM-free:</p> <table> <tr><th>Indicator</th><th>Where to Find It</th><th>What It Means</th></tr> <tr><td>&#8220;sold without Digital Rights Management Software (DRM) applied&#8221;</td><td>Under the main book summary</td><td>Publisher explicitly opted out of DRM</td></tr> <tr><td>&#8220;Simultaneous Device Usage: Unlimited&#8221;</td><td>Product Details section</td><td>No device limit enforced — no DRM</td></tr> <tr><td>Available as EPUB or PDF download</td><td>Download options page (post-purchase)</td><td>You can download the actual EPUB file</td></tr> </table> <h3>Method 2: Known DRM-Free Publishers on Kindle</h3> <p>As of July 2026, the majority of DRM-free Kindle books come from a small but committed group of publishers. <strong>TOR Books</strong> (Macmillan's science fiction and fantasy imprint) and <strong>Baen Publishing</strong> have offered DRM-free ebooks for years. Many TOR novellas, short story collections, and backlist titles are available without DRM on Kindle. Baen's entire Webscription catalog is DRM-free across all platforms, including Kindle.</p> <p>Beyond these two, a growing number of independent authors on Kindle Direct Publishing (KDP) are choosing the DRM-free option for their new releases. Self-published authors in genres like literary fiction, poetry, and non-fiction are the most likely to opt out of DRM, often because they distribute the same book across multiple platforms (Kobo, Google Play Books, Apple Books) and want to keep the file uniform.</p> <h3>Method 3: Use the DRM-Free Filter in Search (If Available)</h3> <p>As of publication, Amazon has not added a dedicated DRM-Free filter to Kindle Store search results. The indicators are only visible on individual product pages. This may change in future iterations, but for now, the most efficient approach is to search for authors and publishers who are known to offer DRM-free EPUBs on Kindle.</p> <h2>How to Download DRM-Free Kindle Books as EPUB</h2> <p>Once you have identified a DRM-free Kindle book and purchased it, downloading the EPUB file is straightforward:</p> <ol> <li><strong>Go to Your Content and Devices</strong> on Amazon's website (Accounts & Lists > Content & Devices).</li> <li>Find the book in your library. DRM-free titles will show both a "Download & transfer via USB" option and an <strong>EPUB download</strong> option.</li> <li>Click the download button next to the book and choose <strong>"EPUB"</strong> from the format dropdown.</li> <li>The EPUB file downloads to your computer as a standard .epub file with no DRM encryption.</li> </ol> <p>That is it. No additional tools, no DRM removal scripts, no Calibre plugins. The file you download is a proper EPUB 3 file that any EPUB reader can open.</p> <p><strong>Important:</strong> This only works for books where the publisher or author has explicitly opted out of DRM. If you do not see the DRM-Free indicator on the product page, the EPUB download option will either be absent or the file will still carry Amazon's DRM encryption.</p> <h2>How to Transfer DRM-Free EPUBs to Any Device</h2> <p>Once you have the DRM-free EPUB file, transferring it to your preferred reading device is identical to sideloading any other EPUB. Here are the quickest paths for the most popular devices:</p> <table> <tr><th>Device</th><th>Transfer Method</th><th>Notes</th></tr> <tr><td>Kobo (Clara, Libra, Sage, Elipsa)</td><td>USB transfer to the main folder, or Calibre wireless sync</td><td>Kobo reads EPUB natively. No conversion needed.</td></tr> <tr><td>PocketBook (Era, InkPad, Touch)</td><td>USB, Dropbox, Send-to-PocketBook email</td><td>PocketBook reads EPUB natively with Adobe RMSDK or own engine.</td></tr> <tr><td>Boox (Poke, Note, Tab, Go)</td><td>USB, Google Drive, Dropbox, NeoReader</td><td>Boox runs Android — any EPUB reader app works.</td></tr> <tr><td>Kindle (any jailbroken model)</td><td>USB or Calibre with KOReader</td><td>Stock Kindle firmware does not read EPUB; install KOReader or convert via Calibre.</td></tr> <tr><td>Phone/Tablet</td><td>Email, cloud storage, USB</td><td>Use any EPUB reader app (see our <a href="/blog/best-epub-reader-apps-2026/">best EPUB reader apps guide</a>).</td></tr> </table> <p>If you manage a large library, <a href="/blog/calibre-library-management-guide-2026/">Calibre is the best tool for organizing, tagging, and syncing</a> your DRM-free EPUB collection across multiple devices. Since these files have no DRM, Calibre can read the metadata, convert between formats if needed, and sync wirelessly to Kobo devices via the Kobo Touch Extended driver.</p> <h2>What This Means for Your EPUB Workflow</h2> <p>The DRM-free indicator is more than a convenience — it signals a structural shift in how Amazon approaches the ebook market. Three trends are converging:</p> <ul> <li><strong>Author choice:</strong> KDP authors can now opt out of DRM at the book level, giving them direct control over how readers access their work.</li> <li><strong>Format standardization:</strong> By accepting EPUB as the primary upload format (replacing the legacy MOBI/AZW pipeline), Amazon has aligned with the industry standard.</li> <li><strong>Reader expectations:</strong> The "Not buying another Kindle" discussion on Hacker News (330+ points) and the proliferation of third-party EPUB reading apps show that readers increasingly expect platform independence.</li> </ul> <p>For the EPUB reader, the practical implication is clear: you no longer need to choose between Amazon's catalog and device freedom. A DRM-free Kindle book can go straight into your Kobo library, your Calibre collection, or your Boox reading folder with zero friction.</p> <h2>Limitations to Keep in Mind</h2> <p>It is important to be realistic about what this change is and is not:</p> <ul> <li><strong>Not all books are DRM-free.</strong> The majority of Kindle titles, especially from the Big Five publishers, still carry DRM. The indicator only appears when the publisher explicitly opts out.</li> <li><strong>No store-wide filter yet.</strong> Amazon has not added a DRM-free search filter, so finding eligible titles requires checking product pages individually or following known DRM-free publishers.</li> <li><strong>Older books need manual opt-in.</strong> For books published before December 9, 2025, authors and publishers must log into KDP and manually confirm the DRM-free setting. Many have not done this yet.</li> <li><strong>Historical KDP back-catalog.</strong> Even for books published after the December 2025 deadline, the DRM-free option must be explicitly chosen during the book submission process. It is not automatic.</li> </ul> <h2>How Authors Can Offer DRM-Free Books on KDP</h2> <p>If you are a KDP author reading this, the process for enabling DRM-free EPUB downloads is simple:</p> <ol> <li>Log into <strong>KDP.amazon.com</strong> and go to your Bookshelf.</li> <li>Edit the book you want to make DRM-free (or create a new title).</li> <li>In the <strong>Digital Rights Management</strong> section under Pricing, select <strong>"Disable DRM"</strong> for the Kindle edition.</li> <li>Upload your EPUB file (KDP now accepts EPUB natively — no need to convert via KindleGen).</li> <li>Publish. Your book will display the DRM-Free indicator on its Kindle Store product page within 24-48 hours.</li> </ol> <p>For books published before December 9, 2025, you must edit the existing title and manually confirm the DRM-free setting. KDP does not retroactively apply the change to legacy titles.</p> <h2>What to Read Next</h2> <p>If you are building a cross-platform reading setup, these related guides will help you get the most from your EPUB library:</p> <ul> <li><a href="/blog/drm-free-epub-library-2026/">DRM-Free EPUB Library 2026</a> — Where to buy DRM-free ebooks beyond Amazon, and how to organize them with Calibre</li> <li><a href="/blog/amazon-kdp-epub-downloads-2026/">Amazon Now Lets You Download KDP Books as EPUB</a> — The earlier policy shift that made EPUB the standard upload format on KDP</li> <li><a href="/blog/best-epub-reader-apps-2026/">Best EPUB Reader Apps for 2026</a> — KOReader, Thorium Reader, Calibre Viewer, and more for every platform</li> <li><a href="/blog/calibre-library-management-guide-2026/">Calibre EPUB Library Guide</a> — Organize, convert, and sync your growing EPUB collection</li> </ul> <section class="faq" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/FAQPage"> <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2> <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"> <h3 itemprop="name">How do I know if a Kindle book is DRM-free before buying?</h3> <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"> <div itemprop="text"> <p>Check the Product Details section on the Kindle book's page. If you see "At the Publisher's request, this title is being sold without Digital Rights Management Software (DRM) applied" or "Simultaneous Device Usage: Unlimited," the book is DRM-free.</p> </div> </div> </div> <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"> <h3 itemprop="name">Can I download a DRM-free Kindle book as EPUB?</h3> <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"> <div itemprop="text"> <p>Yes. Go to Your Content and Devices on Amazon, find the book, and choose the EPUB download option. The file will be a standard .epub file with no DRM encryption.</p> </div> </div> </div> <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"> <h3 itemprop="name">Can I read a DRM-free Kindle book on Kobo?</h3> <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"> <div itemprop="text"> <p>Yes. Kobo devices read EPUB natively. Just download the DRM-free EPUB file from Amazon and transfer it to your Kobo via USB or Calibre wireless sync. No conversion needed.</p> </div> </div> </div> <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"> <h3 itemprop="name">Can I read a DRM-free Kindle book on a jailbroken Kindle with KOReader?</h3> <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"> <div itemprop="text"> <p>Yes. If your Kindle is jailbroken with KOReader installed, you can copy the DRM-free EPUB file directly to the KOReader books folder. KOReader reads EPUB natively with muPDF rendering, often with better typography than Amazon's stock reader.</p> </div> </div> </div> <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"> <h3 itemprop="name">Which publishers offer DRM-free Kindle books?</h3> <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"> <div itemprop="text"> <p>TOR Books and Baen Publishing are the two largest DRM-free publishers on Kindle. Many independent KDP authors also offer DRM-free titles, particularly in literary fiction, poetry, and non-fiction categories.</p> </div> </div> </div> <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"> <h3 itemprop="name">Can I convert a DRM-free EPUB to Kindle Format (AZW3/KFX)?</h3> <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"> <div itemprop="text"> <p>Yes. Since the file has no DRM, you can use Calibre to convert the EPUB to AZW3 or KFX for a non-jailbroken Kindle. Use the Calibre conversion tool with "output profile: Kindle" for best results.</p> </div> </div> </div> <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"> <h3 itemprop="name">Does Amazon have a DRM-free filter in the Kindle Store?</h3> <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"> <div itemprop="text"> <p>Not yet. As of July 2026, Amazon has not added a dedicated DRM-free filter to Kindle Store search. You must check individual product pages or follow known DRM-free publishers.</p> </div> </div> </div> <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"> <h3 itemprop="name">How do I enable DRM-free on my KDP book?</h3> <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"> <div itemprop="text"> <p>Log into KDP.amazon.com, edit your book, go to the Digital Rights Management section under Pricing, and select "Disable DRM." Upload your EPUB file and publish. The DRM-Free indicator will appear on the Kindle Store product page within 24-48 hours.</p> </div> </div>
      `}/>
